{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target's central confound claim inverts the record's actual denominators. It asserts compile.py is 'the one module where the test-to-mutation ratio is high enough for a guard to have any measurable effect' and that its denominator is 'small enough for a single selective event to dominate'. But compile.py has NINE mutations — the largest denominator named in the problem — while the ordinarily-guarded modules have 4 to 7. If registry/mutation-count dominance were the operative mechanism, nine mutations is precisely the case where one selective event should be diluted (1 of 9), and the claim 'a single shown-to-fail-first installation produces a clean 1-of-9 loss' concedes exactly the result the installation-order explanation predicts for the opposite reason. Worse, the mechanism is internally self-defeating: it claims that with 4–7 mutations 'a guard that merely describes the existing code passes nearly all mutations, producing the observed 4/4 to 6/7 losses' — but a DESCRIBING guard that passes mutants should produce LOW loss, whereas 4/4 to 6/7 are HIGH loss rates (4 of 4, 6 of 7 mutants lost = killed, depending on reading either all-caught or all-escaped). The target never says whether 'lost' means killed or surviving, and its explanation 'guards were not established against an independent rejection set' does not generate the 4/4-to-6/7 uniformity it cites: an explanation in terms of per-module arbitrary mutation counts would predict scattered loss fractions, not a tight band shared by every ordinarily-guarded module. The countercondition 'If compile.py had nine mutations and still lost only one, installation order would be a stronger explanation' describes the record as given — compile.py DID have nine mutations and lost only one — yet the target dismisses installation order anyway, making its own falsifier unfalsifiable in practice.","counterexample":null,"premise":null,"premise_evidence":null}]}